<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitutions of Melfi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1231">1231</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Liber Augustalis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitutions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melfi">Melfi</a> were a law code issued by the Emperor Frederick II for the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> at Melfi in 1231. Drafted by the jurists of his court, they asserted the king's supreme authority, regulated officials, courts and professions such as medicine, and limited the powers of barons and towns. They are counted among the most ambitious legislative works of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitutions-of-melfi.jpg" alt="Constitutions of Melfi" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Mikils assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castello_di_melfi1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
